Forced-subtraction cross-examination (wave 2026-09-17 0730, full report: .scratch/forced-subtraction-2026-09-17-0730.md) verdict: **evaluate-only** — the duplication is real, but nothing guards the change.

**Confirmed duplication (HEAD line numbers).** The viewport edge literal `8` appears as policy in three places: the persisted-offset drag clamp `rapid-feedback.user.js:1319-1320`, automatic placement `gap`/`pad` in placePopover `:1342-1349`, and `pad` in centeredFallbackRect `:1355-1357`. Placement is shared by five paths (open `:1564`/`:2045`, anchor re-projection `:1378`, reset `:1335`, auto `:2248`, drag baseline `:1323`), so a wrong centralization spreads everywhere.

**Top break risks from cross-exam.** Mixing gap vs pad moves the popover by pixels while clamps still look right; clamping the fallback rect or touching its `top: 80` breaks lost-target reset; reordering placePopover branches switches edge targets from below/above to side placement; any caller left on literal `8` keeps the policy split.

**Why not implemented this wave:** there is no focused test for the placement matrix or the centered fallback (run.js/run3/run4/run6/run7/run8 exercise flows, not branch geometry), and placePopover is closure-internal with no API seam to assert against.

**Concrete checkpoint for the next wave:** (1) land a branch-preserving placement test — either a harness seam exposing the geometry helpers to the test page, or UI-level viewport-size cases per branch; (2) only then centralize the 8s (consts or the proposed popoverViewportInsets/clampPopoverPosition helpers), preserving branch order, `top: 80`, and measured height usage; (3) hostile review as usual. Implementation is explicitly NOT the todo until step 1 exists.
